1
00:00:01,304 --> 00:00:01,984
Hi, everyone.

2
00:00:03,014 --> 00:00:06,953
I'm Karan Bansal, and this is
Hardening AI Coding Agents with Hooks.

3
00:00:08,294 --> 00:00:10,594
Here is my one-line pitch
for the next half hour.

4
00:00:11,324 --> 00:00:14,894
The guardrails that actually
hold up around AI coding agents

5
00:00:14,894 --> 00:00:16,174
do not live inside the model.

6
00:00:16,913 --> 00:00:18,504
They live in the harness around it.

7
00:00:20,183 --> 00:00:22,624
This is a field report, not a theory talk.

8
00:00:23,333 --> 00:00:27,213
Everything I show you is open
source, MIT licensed, and

9
00:00:27,224 --> 00:00:28,594
running on my machine right now.

10
00:00:29,294 --> 00:00:30,414
Let's get into it

11
00:00:31,414 --> 00:00:34,674
Quick credentials so you can
calibrate how much to trust me.

12
00:00:35,514 --> 00:00:39,524
I am head of AI at Armorcode
with ten plus years in security.

13
00:00:40,184 --> 00:00:42,954
Founding engineer at Avid
Secure, which Sophos acquired.

14
00:00:43,694 --> 00:00:46,744
Before that, I ran security
and privacy at Urban Company.

15
00:00:47,424 --> 00:00:49,914
I have spoken at DEF
CON and OWASP chapters.

16
00:00:50,904 --> 00:00:54,203
The line that actually matters
for this talk is the last one.

17
00:00:55,074 --> 00:00:58,624
I maintain Claude Codebooks,
the open source repo everything

18
00:00:58,624 --> 00:00:59,964
in this talk is built on.

19
00:01:01,173 --> 00:01:03,854
Four hundred and seventy-six
stars as of August 18th.

20
00:01:04,784 --> 00:01:07,253
The table on the right is
my other open source work.

21
00:01:08,253 --> 00:01:12,383
One rule for every number you will
see today, pulled fresh, never from

22
00:01:12,383 --> 00:01:15,253
memory Here is the problem in one line.

23
00:01:16,153 --> 00:01:19,353
An AI coding agent is a
developer with no judgment.

24
00:01:20,953 --> 00:01:22,803
Think about what we handed on day one.

25
00:01:23,413 --> 00:01:29,463
Your shell, your files, web fetch,
MCP servers, Git Push, and it can

26
00:01:29,473 --> 00:01:31,373
spawn sub-agents so it recurses.

27
00:01:32,083 --> 00:01:35,803
Every single move is the model's
call, and it happens at machine

28
00:01:35,803 --> 00:01:38,273
speed, dozens of actions a minute.

29
00:01:39,133 --> 00:01:41,423
Now put that next to the adoption numbers.

30
00:01:42,133 --> 00:01:45,263
Ninety percent of developers
already use AI at work.

31
00:01:45,833 --> 00:01:47,337
Only twenty-nine percent trust the output.

32
00:01:47,337 --> 00:01:50,945
And Vera Code's compilation puts
a vulnerability in forty-five

33
00:01:50,945 --> 00:01:53,713
percent of AI-generated code.

34
00:01:55,403 --> 00:01:58,683
So we are handing write access
to something we do not trust.

35
00:01:59,113 --> 00:02:04,753
That gap between what we grant and what we
trust is what this talk is about closing

36
00:02:05,753 --> 00:02:08,433
Four dates from this summer
tell you exactly where we are.

37
00:02:09,373 --> 00:02:14,623
August 4th, the Chaindrop worm
backdoors more than 400 npm packages.

38
00:02:15,183 --> 00:02:17,353
And here is the detail
that matters for this talk.

39
00:02:18,343 --> 00:02:21,983
It persists by planting a session
start hook in .cloud/settings.json.

40
00:02:22,983 --> 00:02:27,093
The agent's own config file, a
file security scanners never read.

41
00:02:27,933 --> 00:02:31,343
The worm weaponized the exact
mechanism this talk is about.

42
00:02:33,173 --> 00:02:38,053
July 16, Hugging Face discloses
an end-to-end breach run not by a

43
00:02:38,073 --> 00:02:42,793
human operator, but by an autonomous
agent swarm at machine speed.

44
00:02:43,313 --> 00:02:47,033
June and July, the Cursor
Dune slide CVs go public.

45
00:02:47,573 --> 00:02:49,533
CVSS 9.8.

46
00:02:49,533 --> 00:02:53,223
Zero-click prompt injection all
the way to OS-level code execution.

47
00:02:53,913 --> 00:02:58,403
One flaw overrode the sandbox's
own helper binary, the other

48
00:02:58,423 --> 00:02:59,803
broke symlink resolution.

49
00:03:00,853 --> 00:03:06,583
And on August 3rd and 4th, the OS
ships the LLM Top 10 for 2026 with

50
00:03:06,643 --> 00:03:10,973
excessive agency, the risk this whole
talk is about, up at number three.

51
00:03:12,493 --> 00:03:17,953
This talk got easier to justify
and harder to ignore Before any

52
00:03:17,953 --> 00:03:19,943
defense makes sense, one concept.

53
00:03:20,733 --> 00:03:24,553
Every AI coding agent from
every vendor runs the same loop.

54
00:03:25,653 --> 00:03:27,673
Think, the model picks an action.

55
00:03:28,333 --> 00:03:31,243
Act, the harness executes the tool call.

56
00:03:31,793 --> 00:03:34,823
Observe, the result goes
back into the context window.

57
00:03:35,423 --> 00:03:37,693
Then repeat dozens of times a minute.

58
00:03:38,963 --> 00:03:40,843
Here is the part most people miss.

59
00:03:41,293 --> 00:03:42,713
The model is a black box.

60
00:03:42,893 --> 00:03:46,623
You cannot patch policy into its
weights, and prompt instructions

61
00:03:46,623 --> 00:03:48,523
are suggestions, not controls.

62
00:03:49,243 --> 00:03:53,363
Anyone who has watched an agent cheerfully
ignores its system prompt knows this.

63
00:03:55,043 --> 00:04:00,823
So security has to happen between the
steps, between act and observe, at the

64
00:04:00,823 --> 00:04:03,663
exact moment a decision becomes an action.

65
00:04:05,033 --> 00:04:08,333
That seam is where everything
else in this talk lives

66
00:04:09,333 --> 00:04:10,613
Now the reality check.

67
00:04:11,113 --> 00:04:14,923
The textbook's answer is a
sandbox, and the textbook is right.

68
00:04:15,533 --> 00:04:18,233
But in practice, almost nobody runs one.

69
00:04:19,053 --> 00:04:21,693
People launch agents
with skip permissions.

70
00:04:21,843 --> 00:04:23,713
They click Allow All by minute three.

71
00:04:24,603 --> 00:04:27,503
They work in their main dev
environment because the sandbox

72
00:04:27,563 --> 00:04:29,423
breaks CI and half of their tooling.

73
00:04:30,863 --> 00:04:34,263
And lately, the story got worse because
even the shipped sandboxes failed.

74
00:04:34,263 --> 00:04:38,763
Duneslide overrode the
sandbox's own helper binary.

75
00:04:38,763 --> 00:04:44,753
Cursor had a GitHub code
execution flaw, CVE-2026-26268.

76
00:04:46,523 --> 00:04:51,283
And the Pocket OS incident, an agent
holding one overbroad railway token

77
00:04:51,283 --> 00:04:54,803
deleted a production database and
its backups in about nine seconds.

78
00:04:56,633 --> 00:04:57,633
Nine seconds.

79
00:04:57,813 --> 00:05:00,373
That is the machine
speed part made concrete.

80
00:05:01,373 --> 00:05:02,763
So here is where the hooks sit.

81
00:05:03,363 --> 00:05:05,973
If you skip sandboxes, hooks
are your enforcement layer.

82
00:05:06,493 --> 00:05:08,413
If you don't, hooks are your second layer.

83
00:05:08,973 --> 00:05:10,293
Either way, you want them.

84
00:05:11,293 --> 00:05:12,953
So here is the architecture.

85
00:05:13,823 --> 00:05:15,093
Hooks are checkpoints.

86
00:05:15,903 --> 00:05:18,233
The harness fires at
fixed points in that loop.

87
00:05:19,083 --> 00:05:22,213
On the left is the full life
cycle diagram from my blog post.

88
00:05:23,103 --> 00:05:27,683
The middle panel zooms into the agentic
loop where the decisions actually happen.

89
00:05:28,683 --> 00:05:35,593
Five hook types: Command, HTTP,
MCP Tool, Prompt, and Agent.

90
00:05:36,493 --> 00:05:38,633
And the contract is deliberately tiny.

91
00:05:39,373 --> 00:05:41,403
Exit code two blocks the action.

92
00:05:41,993 --> 00:05:44,773
Exit zero with JSON response
or structured decision.

93
00:05:45,963 --> 00:05:50,823
Hooks run in parallel, synchronization by
default, asynchronous when you flag that.

94
00:05:52,393 --> 00:05:55,323
I want to stress how
boring that contract is.

95
00:05:55,813 --> 00:05:58,443
JSON in, JSON out, exit codes.

96
00:05:59,233 --> 00:06:01,133
That boringness is a feature.

97
00:06:01,753 --> 00:06:05,403
It is exactly why every major
vendor converged on the same shape,

98
00:06:05,593 --> 00:06:06,763
which I'll show you near the end

99
00:06:07,763 --> 00:06:13,043
Claude port now exposes
31 hook events, 31.

100
00:06:13,043 --> 00:06:14,223
You do not need all of them.

101
00:06:14,503 --> 00:06:15,983
Six carry the security load.

102
00:06:17,983 --> 00:06:20,863
Pre_tool_use is the big
one, the enforcement point

103
00:06:20,883 --> 00:06:22,403
before anything executes.

104
00:06:22,943 --> 00:06:26,013
If you remember one event name
from today, make it that one.

105
00:06:27,003 --> 00:06:29,323
Post_tool_use inspects
results after the call.

106
00:06:29,983 --> 00:06:34,013
User_prompt_submit screens input
before the model ever sees it.

107
00:06:35,243 --> 00:06:37,763
Permission_request intercepts
privilege escalation.

108
00:06:38,183 --> 00:06:41,913
Instructions loaded watches context
files as they load into the session.

109
00:06:43,423 --> 00:06:45,593
And the last one, config_change.

110
00:06:46,353 --> 00:06:48,473
It earned its place on
the list this summer.

111
00:06:49,163 --> 00:06:51,573
Config files became an attack surface.

112
00:06:51,833 --> 00:06:53,313
Chaindrop made sure of that.

113
00:06:54,163 --> 00:06:57,033
Who guards the guards is now
a real question, and we'll get

114
00:06:57,063 --> 00:06:58,573
to it properly in a few slides

115
00:06:59,573 --> 00:07:03,973
Let's look at the anatomy of one call
because this slide is the whole mechanism.

116
00:07:05,463 --> 00:07:09,713
Left slide, the harness pipes
JSON to your hook on stdin.

117
00:07:11,613 --> 00:07:16,233
Tool name, the full arguments,
session ID, working directory.

118
00:07:17,183 --> 00:07:21,643
In this example, the agent wants
to run rm -rf on a projects folder.

119
00:07:22,333 --> 00:07:27,573
Right side, your hook writes JSON
back, permission decision, deny,

120
00:07:28,243 --> 00:07:30,113
plus a reason in plain language.

121
00:07:31,073 --> 00:07:34,853
That reason gets fed back to the
model, so it adapts instead of

122
00:07:34,863 --> 00:07:36,443
blindly retrying the same thing.

123
00:07:38,253 --> 00:07:40,493
Now notice what is absent from the slide.

124
00:07:41,093 --> 00:07:43,793
No prompt, no LLM in the middle.

125
00:07:44,573 --> 00:07:50,363
The hook is a separate process the
model never sees, which means it cannot

126
00:07:50,373 --> 00:07:54,733
be jailbroken, and it cannot be social
engineered from inside the prompt.

127
00:07:56,373 --> 00:08:00,563
That one property is what everything
else in this talk builds on

128
00:08:01,563 --> 00:08:06,763
Two weeks before this airs, OWASP
shipped the 2026 LLM Top 10.

129
00:08:07,473 --> 00:08:09,883
So let's key the whole
story to the new list.

130
00:08:10,493 --> 00:08:12,563
Same 10 risks, new order

131
00:08:13,563 --> 00:08:17,933
The moves that matter here, excessive
agency jumped from six to three,

132
00:08:18,733 --> 00:08:23,843
unbounded consumption climbed from 10
to six, improper output handling fell

133
00:08:23,843 --> 00:08:28,943
from five all the way to 10, and system
prompt leakage got renamed to hidden

134
00:08:28,943 --> 00:08:34,373
context exposure with a wider scope One
caveat before you quote me, the claim

135
00:08:34,373 --> 00:08:38,093
that the Sri rank was weighted by real
incident data is secondary sourced.

136
00:08:38,583 --> 00:08:44,493
Verify it against the official PDF before
repeating it My scorecard stays honest.

137
00:08:44,953 --> 00:08:47,773
Six of the 10 have a
hook-shaped answer today.

138
00:08:48,513 --> 00:08:51,993
Five covered, and the top
row says bounded on purpose.

139
00:08:52,813 --> 00:08:55,493
Nobody reliably prevents prompt injection.

140
00:08:56,123 --> 00:08:59,513
Hooks decide what an injected
agent still gets to do.

141
00:09:00,213 --> 00:09:01,163
One is conditional.

142
00:09:02,243 --> 00:09:05,253
Three are genuinely out of scope
at runtime, and I would rather

143
00:09:05,273 --> 00:09:06,893
tell you that than pretend.

144
00:09:08,213 --> 00:09:11,453
The headline, the list moved
towards the tool boundary, which

145
00:09:11,453 --> 00:09:13,703
is exactly where the hooks sit

146
00:09:14,703 --> 00:09:18,983
OWASP also gave agentic risks
their own list in December, the

147
00:09:18,983 --> 00:09:20,963
top 10 for agentic applications.

148
00:09:21,813 --> 00:09:25,583
If the LLM list is about models,
this one is about actions.

149
00:09:26,833 --> 00:09:32,763
Hooks land squarely on the four
highlighted entries, ASI-02 Tool Misuse,

150
00:09:32,763 --> 00:09:39,963
ASI-03 Identity and Privilege Abuse,
ASI-05 Unexpected Code Execution, and

151
00:09:39,963 --> 00:09:42,813
ASI-09 Human Agent Trust Exploitation.

152
00:09:44,793 --> 00:09:49,683
And note the line at the bottom,
OWASP's own aly- analysis says prompt

153
00:09:49,683 --> 00:09:51,413
injection touches six of these 10.

154
00:09:52,523 --> 00:09:56,903
Prompt injection is not getting solved
this year, so the practical move is not

155
00:09:57,163 --> 00:10:01,493
preventing injection, it is bounding
what an injected agent is allowed to do.

156
00:10:03,233 --> 00:10:07,103
Which brings us to the hooks themselves,
real code, shipped and tested

157
00:10:08,103 --> 00:10:10,363
First shipped hook,
block dangerous commands.

158
00:10:10,363 --> 00:10:13,103
A pre-tool use hook on Bash.

159
00:10:13,493 --> 00:10:16,933
What you are looking at is the
actual pattern table from the repo.

160
00:10:17,693 --> 00:10:23,573
Each entry has an ID, a regex,
and a human-readable reason.

161
00:10:23,693 --> 00:10:26,883
Rm targeting your home
directory, DD writing to a

162
00:10:26,883 --> 00:10:28,933
disk device, a fork bomb, curl

163
00:10:29,933 --> 00:10:30,053
piped into a shell.

164
00:10:30,053 --> 00:10:31,123
Three safety levels.

165
00:10:31,313 --> 00:10:33,393
Critical stops catastrophes only.

166
00:10:33,393 --> 00:10:36,963
High adds risky operations
like Git reset hard.

167
00:10:37,463 --> 00:10:39,193
Strict adds the cautionary tier.

168
00:10:39,853 --> 00:10:44,113
And you set the level per install with
an environment variable, no code edit.

169
00:10:45,283 --> 00:10:49,593
And my favorite part, graduated
ask mode arrived as a community PR.

170
00:10:50,593 --> 00:10:54,913
Instead of a flat deny, a level can
escalate to the human, so you decide

171
00:10:54,933 --> 00:10:56,423
the gray zone instead of the model.

172
00:10:57,983 --> 00:11:01,113
On the twenty twenty-six
list, this is LLM zero three.

173
00:11:01,713 --> 00:11:04,213
Excessive agency, up three places.

174
00:11:04,783 --> 00:11:06,373
And the chip in the corner is real.

175
00:11:07,913 --> 00:11:11,443
One thousand five hundred seventy
tests, green on August thirteen.

176
00:11:12,043 --> 00:11:14,933
Zero NPM dependencies, MIT licensed.

177
00:11:16,773 --> 00:11:18,993
Second shipped hook, product secrets.

178
00:11:19,643 --> 00:11:21,923
Two pattern families
doing complementary work.

179
00:11:22,683 --> 00:11:26,503
File patterns catch reads,
writes, and edits of .env

180
00:11:26,503 --> 00:11:29,753
files, SSH private keys,
and cloud credentials.

181
00:11:30,663 --> 00:11:35,303
Bash patterns catch the sneaky path,
environment dumps, and exfiltration

182
00:11:36,563 --> 00:11:37,773
over curl, SCP, rsync or netcat.

183
00:11:39,073 --> 00:11:41,743
The allow list at the top
matters for usability.

184
00:11:42,873 --> 00:11:44,293
.env.example

185
00:11:44,493 --> 00:11:47,743
and templates stay readable, so
the agent can still do its job.

186
00:11:48,633 --> 00:11:53,003
No LLM, no network call, pure
regex and one script covers

187
00:11:53,043 --> 00:11:55,303
read, edit, write, and bash.

188
00:11:56,193 --> 00:12:01,403
This hook alone carries one forty-five
tests as of August thirteen repo audit.

189
00:12:02,603 --> 00:12:05,783
If your rule is that an agent should
never read your credentials, this is

190
00:12:05,783 --> 00:12:08,593
about as cheap as real enforcement gets

191
00:12:09,593 --> 00:12:13,123
Now, the genuinely new attack
surface, and if you take one new

192
00:12:13,123 --> 00:12:14,933
idea from this talk, take this one.

193
00:12:16,433 --> 00:12:20,963
Your agent's config files
are now malware carriers.

194
00:12:22,273 --> 00:12:26,963
Trapdoor plants zero-width Unicode
instructions inside claude.md

195
00:12:27,463 --> 00:12:29,103
and .cursor

196
00:12:29,103 --> 00:12:31,563
rules files invisible to a human reviewer.

197
00:12:32,173 --> 00:12:35,603
Detection averaged five minutes
and fifty-six seconds after

198
00:12:35,673 --> 00:12:37,623
publish, and it still landed.

199
00:12:38,803 --> 00:12:45,363
Chain drop from slide four plants its
persistent hook in .claude/settings.json.

200
00:12:46,083 --> 00:12:52,593
And CVE-2026-25725 showed
sandboxed code injecting persistent

201
00:12:52,633 --> 00:12:54,293
hooks through settings.json

202
00:12:54,363 --> 00:12:54,763
itself.

203
00:12:55,713 --> 00:13:00,303
The attack configures the defender
The hook answers ship this week.

204
00:13:00,783 --> 00:13:05,523
Config Guard blocks the agent rewriting
its own guardrails at pre-tool use,

205
00:13:06,053 --> 00:13:10,043
and Config Watch writes the config
change event for everything else.

206
00:13:11,033 --> 00:13:15,583
Watch the files that configure your
agent, including the hook config itself.

207
00:13:16,473 --> 00:13:21,333
The platform answer is Workspace Trust,
which gates hooks in untrusted folders as

208
00:13:21,343 --> 00:13:23,653
of version two dot one dot two one eight.

209
00:13:25,603 --> 00:13:26,693
And the chip is current.

210
00:13:27,223 --> 00:13:28,573
Both hooks are in the repo.

211
00:13:28,853 --> 00:13:30,143
Code you can run today

212
00:13:31,143 --> 00:13:35,313
This is my favorite hook in the repo
because it targets agent behavior

213
00:13:35,523 --> 00:13:37,123
rather than attacker behavior.

214
00:13:38,413 --> 00:13:39,553
Here is the failure mode.

215
00:13:40,143 --> 00:13:44,153
An agent under pressure to make the build
green will sometimes to just delete the

216
00:13:44,163 --> 00:13:49,703
failing test or rename it out of the
test globe or slap a skip marker on it.

217
00:13:50,223 --> 00:13:54,083
The build goes green, the evidence goes
away, and you find out in the production.

218
00:13:54,733 --> 00:13:58,923
I call it the fake green problem
Product tests community PR

219
00:13:58,923 --> 00:14:01,233
20 blocks all three moves.

220
00:14:03,113 --> 00:14:06,653
Thirty seconds on its sibling,
case insensitive guard, because

221
00:14:06,683 --> 00:14:10,403
it is such a clean example of
folks knowing what models do not.

222
00:14:11,353 --> 00:14:18,243
On a case insensitive file system
like APFS, RM-RF lowercase content

223
00:14:18,243 --> 00:14:19,903
can take out capital C content.

224
00:14:19,903 --> 00:14:24,523
A hook can know that about your file
system, the model reliably does not.

225
00:14:25,523 --> 00:14:26,393
Hidden context.

226
00:14:26,943 --> 00:14:28,423
This is your claude.md,

227
00:14:28,653 --> 00:14:32,383
your system prompts, your hidden
policy files walking out the door.

228
00:14:33,353 --> 00:14:36,293
OWASP renamed this risk in
two thousand and twenty-six.

229
00:14:37,193 --> 00:14:41,453
System prompt leakage became hidden
content exposure, and the official scope

230
00:14:41,463 --> 00:14:46,013
widened to developer instructions, RAG
schemas, and the hidden policy logic.

231
00:14:47,333 --> 00:14:48,343
Two attack shapes.

232
00:14:49,083 --> 00:14:52,423
One, the instruction you
load get exfiltrated.

233
00:14:53,093 --> 00:14:57,353
Two, the nastier one, prompt injection
through the file system where

234
00:14:57,353 --> 00:15:00,603
something writes rules into your
config for the next session to obey.

235
